sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch09-04> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "The Second Law of Thermodynamics is observed to hold because practical experiments can only set up initial conditions that are simple to describe and construct, and finding initial conditions of a reversible system that would lead to decreasing randomness requires computation as great as the system's own evolution.";
    dc:type "general-principle";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Second Law holds because experiments cannot prepare randomness-decreasing states";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 9, “Fundamental Physics”; section “Irreversibility and the Second Law of Thermodynamics” (from p. 441), p. 444. Scope: reversible physical systems and the experiments performed on them; origin of the Second Law of Thermodynamics"
}
